In this chapter we would like to summarize the recently reported data on crystal plasticity of the laminated T/M structure of Cu-8at.%Al alloy [ 15 ] in a way useful for the experimental analysis performed later in this work. To consider mechanical properties of a dislocated T/M structure, let us first introduce crystallographic description of the elementary FCC lattice shears of the bimodal T/M unit (Fig.  1 ). The elementary shears of the twin and matrix lattices are shown in Fig.  1 a by means of the tetrahedra, A T B T C T D T  and ABCD, respectively. Following the concept of Thompson’s tetrahedron [ 16 ], specific slip system of the matrix layer can be labeled for instance as AII or CV. The letters A and C as the apex points of the matrix tetrahedron represent the close packed (slip) planes BCD and ABD and the Roman numbers II and V represent the close packed (slip) directions CD and AD, respectively. The corresponding slip systems of the twin layer can be consequently labeled as A T II T  and C T V T , respectively, whereas the twin shear directions are labeled by Arabic numbers. Specific twin system of the matrix layer parallel to the T/M interface (K 1  plane) is labeled, for instance, as C3 and their reciprocal correspondence in the twin layer as C T 3 T . For simplicity, the lattice shear within the twin layer will be later labeled mostly by one superscript “T” only, e.g., A T II T  ≡ AII T  and C T 3 T  ≡ C3 T . It is well known that each of the twelve FCC slip systems can operate forth and back (24 lattice shears), whereas each of the twelve FCC twin systems can operate only in one direction (12 lattice shears). Comparing Fig.  1 b, c one can easily find an exact crystallographic description of each of the 36 elementary lattice shears of the matrix layer and another 36 shears of the twin layer. For instance, slip shear of the twin layer labeled as DIV T  (Fig.  1 b) will have its crystallographic correspondence in Fig.  1 c as ( 1 1 ¯ \documentclass[12pt