				\begin{document}$$\overline{21 }$$\end{document} ) direction, i.e., to the opposite to η 1  direction of primary deformation twinning. Moreover, the correspondence matrix method predicts that the amount of tensile deformation of ε ≈ 0.16 and ε ≈ 0.08 is enough to complete the T/M → M transformation in the case of sample 4 and 2, respectively (note in Fig.  2 c extremely low values of initial Schmid factors of C3 T  detwinning shear). To conclude, complete detwinning of deformation twin lamella creates a well-known situation in plasticity of FCC single crystals, where the tensile instability is premature by entry of a new dominant, non-coplanar, slip system [ 29 ,  30 ,  31 ,  32 ,  33 ,  34 ,  35 – 36 ]. The non-coplanar lattice shear nucleated at high stresses leads always to a heavy strain localization in a deformed crystal structure. Fundamental physics of the phenomenon called as crystal plastic deformation in an “alien” dislocation distribution and referred to the latent hardening of FCC crystals was well established in considerable number of previously published papers [ 38 ,  39 ,  40 ,  41 ,  42 – 43 ]. The premature tensile instability caused by the CI T/M  → PIII or CIII T/M  → PI transformation, as discussed earlier, does certainly obey the same physics of crystal plastic deformation; however, in this case the presence of the T/M interfacial dislocations should be additionally included. This physical background seems also applicable to explain the tensile strengthening properties of samples 1, 16, 17, and 18 characterized by “zero” product of σ UTS  × ε UTS  or σ T  × ε T . These products were formally evaluated as being slightly above 10 MPa, i.e., in each case ε UTS  was established as equal to 0.02 (Table  2 ). As expected from 1/σ map (Fig.  2 b), heavy mechanical instabilities (dσ/dε <  < 0) nucleated at the very beginning of the tensile deformation (for instance see in Fig.  6 a the stress–strain curve of sample 1) were caused by entry of the PI shear mode of the T/M lattice, i.e., a non-coplanar shear with respect to any other lattice shear used in the deformation history of the T/M structure, investigated here (see crystallography of the composition plane of the PI super slip system in Table  1 ). To conclude, due to a discontinued change of the tensile deformation path, a change of the dominant deformation system occurred effectively in samples 1, 16, 17, and 18. Since the changeover was triggered in each case at an extremely high level of the tensile stress (Table  2 ), it led immediately to the sample failure. It is worth mentioning that the case of mechanical instability generated in sample 18 was intensively studied experimentally in [ 15 ], where crystallography of PI super slip, responsible for sample failure, was carefully established. Moreover, in this experimental work plastic yielding of the T/M structure was also studied under the deformation condition, where the initial compression axis was perpendicular to the T/M interface or inclined by about 10 0  (see the insert in Fig.  2 b showing the stereographic regions of M 1  type lattice shears). It was found that in both cases nucleation of the M 1  type T/M lattice shear (Table  1 ), i.e., non-K 1  plane slip system, led immediately to catastrophic strain localization into shear bands. There is no doubt that such a heavy strain localization nucleated at the extremely high tensile stress of about 900 MPa (see σ Y  surfaces in Fig.  3 a, b) shall lead to extremely heavy tensile instability and immediate necking. Despite of the type of uniaxial stress applied, the initial plastic shear would have to occur again under the presence of an “alien” dislocation distribution. To conclude, irrespective of the amounts of the tensile strain accumulated in a deformed sample the activation of the M 1  or M 3  type lattice shears shall always terminate a tensile deformation process of the T/M structure. The cases of the “non-zero ductility” tensile deformation are possible only when M 2  type T/M lattice shears are brought into operation. As mentioned above in chapter 2, exceptionally large CRRS’s of the non-K 1  plane shears result from presence of extended dislocations of cube type [ 25 ,  26 ], attached to the K 1  type planes within the deformation twin lamella. As concluded in [ 15 ,  20 ], these very sessile configurations of cube dislocations of large density are mainly responsible for exceptional σ Y  anisotropy as well as for any transfer of plastic shear throughout the laminated T/M structure. The contribution of T/M interfacial dislocations to the stress barrier of transfer of any plastic shear across the T/M interface was evaluated as not exceeded more than several percent. Therefore, K 1  plane cube dislocations, as the “alien” dislocations, should predominantly control all the cases of the tensile instability occurring at the very beginning of the plastic tension, i.e., at the yield point (σ UTS  ≡ σ Y) . Another words, they control the minimal (“zero”) tensile strength-ductility combination of the T/M structure, investigated here. However, it is important to conclude that these “alien” dislocations, supported by those created during the “large ductility” tensile deformation paths, control also critically maximal strength-ductility properties of the same T/M structure. From the set of the “alien” dislocations, only the T/M interfacial dislocations could be eventually removed from the T/M structure by full operation of the π detwinning mode, e.g., during the C3 T  path of the tensile deformation. The removal will be possible by a process of dislocation annihilation, where twinning and detwinning partials of strictly opposite sense would have to interact mutually. Operation of π detwinning would also be able to bring the cube dislocations back to their matrix crystal origins, but unable to remove them physically from the detwinned T/M structure, i.e., they will still exist as the “alien” dislocations, but in the detwinned configurations. Eventually operation of π/3 detwinning is not able to retransform the cube dislocations back to their crystal matrix origins as well as to secure the process of annihilation of the T/M interfacial dislocations; the physical aspects of π and π/3 detwinning of the FCC deformation twins were thoroughly discussed in [ 44 ]. It is worth mentioning that in case of a polycrystalline material, annihilation of the T/M interfacial dislocations seems rather improbable because of unavoidable interactions of detwinning dislocations with grain boundaries. To summarize the experimental results obtained in this work, tensile strength and ductility of the T/M multilayered Cu-8at%Al crystal structure depend on crystal orientation of the tensile axis in the three distinct ways: (I) when the T/M structure was loaded by tension parallel or almost parallel to the T/M interface, it revealed the effect of “zero” tensile ductility accompanied by relatively high level of true tensile stress of about 550 MPa; plastic yielding occurred by entry of the M 3  type T/M lattice shear; (II) when the T/M structure was loaded by tension perpendicular or almost perpendicular to the T/M interface, it revealed also the effect of “zero” tensile ductility, but it was accompanied by extremely high level of true tensile stress of about 900 MPa; plastic yielding occurred by entry of the M 1  type lattice shear; and (III) when the T/M structure was loaded by tension at an angle inclined by about π/4 to the T/M interface, it revealed the effect of “large” ductility of even to unity of the true tensile strain accompanied by extremely high level of true tensile stress of about 850 MPa at the end of the tensile deformation; plastic yielding occurred by entry of the M 2  type T/M lattice shear. Considering that detwinning restrains tensile ductility of the T/M structure and analyzing the 1/σ Y  map (Fig.  5 b), one can conclude that the most stressed slip systems (0.3 < m < 0.5, Fig.  2 c) of the M 2  group T/M lattice shears are largely responsible for the exceptional tensile strength–ductility combination. The tensile deformation properties of randomly textured polycrystalline materials can be evaluated by means of an average orientation factor m [ 45 ,  46 ]. If the data provided in Table  2  for samples numbered 6, 7, 8, 12, 13, and 14 can on average be representative, the tensile strength–ductility properties of a mechanically twinned Cu-8at%Al polycrystalline structure will be as follows: σ T  ≈ 700 MPa, ε T  ≈ 0.8, and the product σ T  × ε T  ≈ 560 MPa. Consequently, TWIP steels of Kirchhoff’s elastic constant larger by a factor of two, compared to that of the Cu-8at%Al alloy, should reach σ T  ≈ 1.4 GPa, and the product σ T  × ε T  ≈ 1.1 GPa. There is a reasonable agreement of the σ T  and ε T  values predicted with those checked experimentally for the high manganese Hadfield steel, e.g., σ T  = 2 GPa, ε T  = 0.6, and σ T  × ε T  = 1.2 GPa [ 10 ]. Considering the above discussion on the “alien” dislocations and the fact that cube dislocations, consisting of double layer K 1  plane stacking faults, are extremely difficult obstacles to be penetrated by the non-K 1  plane shears [ 47 ,  48 ], we would like to introduce a postulate: strong anisotropic stress field inside the twin lamella, caused by presence of the extended cube dislocations, is mainly responsible for exceptional anisotropy of the strength-ductility properties of the laminated T/M structures.

